# Only two kinds of thing stay out of this repository now: a secret, and
# anything git would be storing a second copy of.
#
# The user asked for the whole project to be backed up, so the databases,
# the traces and the QA artefacts are all tracked. They are the system's own
# history - what it was asked, what it answered, what it cost - and that
# history exists on one disk otherwise.

# 1. The secret. A live API key in git history is permanent even after a
#    force-push, and this file is regenerated from the key you hold anyway.
.env
config.local.toml

# 2. Derived from files that ARE tracked, so storing it is storing it twice.
#    .venv is 138 MB of Windows-only binaries that uv.lock rebuilds exactly,
#    and a Windows venv does not run on the Linux box CI uses.
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/
.coverage
htmlcov/
node_modules/
package-lock.json

# 3. SQLite's own scratch files. The databases in data/ ARE tracked on purpose,
#    but WAL mode writes a -wal journal and a -shm shared-memory block beside
#    each one. They belong to whichever process currently holds the database
#    open, are empty once it closes cleanly, and differ on every machine.
#    Committing a stale -wal is worse than useless: it can confuse the next
#    process that opens the file. Anything that reads a database - a test run,
#    a sweep, `ask.py alerts` - creates them, so without this they reappear as
#    untracked noise after every command.
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# 4. The nightly pack is regenerated from the stores by `ask.py pack`; the
#    page written from it (<date>.md, <date>.json) is the record and IS tracked.
knowledge/feedback/*.pack.md
knowledge/paper/*.pack.md

# The paid embedding cache: vectors for text, rebuildable from the API and
# meaningless without the key. Not a store the system reasons over.
data/embeddings.db
